  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1240 PM CDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nUpper level ridging will continue to dominate the forecast \nthrough the end of this week and into the next. High temperatures \nwill generally remain steady in the upper 90s/lower 100s, but \ncould increase a couple degrees over the weekend as the ridge \nintensifies overhead. All in all, dangerous heat will persist over\nthe next several days with daily heat index values greater than \n105 degrees. Some areas of NE OK may need to be upgraded to a \nwarning for Thursday, but given the potential for stronger \nafternoon winds to help mix out dewpoints more, as well as reduce\nthe WBGT impact slightly, will hold off for now. Otherwise, the \nHeat Advisory was extended until 8 PM Sunday as dangerous heat \nlooks likely into early next week. Additional extensions (partial\nor full) may be required over the coming days. As mentioned \nabove, breezy afternoon SW winds will continue, especially \nThursday and Friday. This may promote a limited fire weather \nthreat given the continued dry and hot conditions.\n\nGuidance continues to suggest potential for a weak frontal \nboundary to move into at least northern parts of the area Monday. \nIf the front did manage to push into our area, it appears there \nwould be a brief window for some low rain chances over NE OK and \nNW AR, as well as a slight cool down. However, given the forecast \nstrength of the ridge (~597dam) and recent model behavior, there \nis reason to be skeptical. For now, the theme of hot and dry will \npersist for the foreseeable future.",
